    "content": "# Pillar Model - Gold Standard Tree\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Gold Standard Tree is the official six-pillar model for organizing Block Ops knowledge, delivery, and future Wiki navigation.\n\nIt explains the full system Block Ops builds around nerve blocks: physical workflow, digital support, human training, stakeholder alignment, value proof, and implementation materials.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nBlock Ops can easily look like many separate things:\n\n- consulting\n- clinical education\n- protocols\n- carts and supplies\n- nursing training\n- documentation templates\n- patient materials\n- dashboards\n- implementation bundles\n- outcome tracking\n\nThe pillar model keeps those pieces organized as one system.\n\nIt prevents the Wiki, sales story, Mission Control, and client-delivery work from drifting into disconnected folders or one-off projects.\n\n## Official six-pillar structure\n\n### 1. Physical Operations \u2014 The Engine\n\nPhysical Operations covers the real-world environment where blocks happen.\n\nIncludes:\n\n- block bay setup\n- workflow logic\n- scheduling and timing\n- ultrasound/equipment readiness\n- cart setup and par levels\n- supply chain\n- cleaning and restocking\n- physical reference materials\n\nCore question:\n\n> Does the site have the physical setup and workflow needed to make nerve blocks reliable day to day?\n\n### 2. Digital Platform \u2014 The System\n\nDigital Platform covers the software, data, and digital reference layer that supports the program.\n\nIncludes:\n\n- internal Dashboard\n- Mission Control\n- client portal\n- approved content library\n- mobile/digital references\n- QR code library\n- CRM and lead tracking\n- data capture\n- Supabase / Vercel runtime\n- future agent/search support\n\nCore question:\n\n> Does the team have a digital system that makes Block Ops easier to run, configure, track, and improve?\n\n### 3. Human Capital \u2014 The People\n\nHuman Capital covers the people, roles, training, and governance needed to sustain the program.\n\nIncludes:\n\n- provider education\n- nursing competency\n- block champion development\n- support staff roles\n- training workflows\n- competency checks\n- governance and sustainability\n- escalation and support roles\n\nCore question:\n\n> Do the people involved know their roles and have the training structure needed to keep the program working?\n\n### 4. Stakeholder Integration \u2014 The Partners\n\nStakeholder Integration covers the people around the program whose buy-in affects success.\n\nIncludes:\n\n- surgeons\n- patients\n- referring providers\n- facility leadership\n- block champions\n- compact/expectation setting\n- patient journey materials\n- communication scripts\n- client relationship touchpoints\n\nCore question:\n\n> Are the right stakeholders aligned so nerve blocks are accepted, understood, and supported?\n\n### 5. Value Intelligence \u2014 The Proof\n\nValue Intelligence covers the evidence, measurement, billing, compliance, risk, and business value layer.\n\nIncludes:\n\n- outcome tracking\n- billing and documentation support\n- ROI analysis\n- compliance and legal review\n- program metrics\n- value reporting\n- risk controls\n- reimbursement support\n- claim review and evidence standards\n\nCore question:\n\n> Can the site understand, document, and prove the value and safety of the program over time?\n\n### 6. Implementation Bundles \u2014 The Productized Materials\n\nImplementation Bundles cover the actual deliverables that make Block Ops usable.\n\nIncludes:\n\n- foundation deliverables\n- future block-specific bundles\n- protocols\n- checklists\n- patient education materials\n- nursing tools\n- documentation templates\n- safety references\n- training materials\n- physical print/download packages\n\nCore question:\n\n> What concrete materials does the site receive and use to implement the system?\n\n## How the pillar model should be used\n\nUse the six pillars as the primary visible structure for the Block Ops Wiki.\n\nThe pillars should help organize:\n\n- Wiki pages\n- implementation bundle content\n- Mission Control context links\n- sales narrative\n- delivery planning\n- client portal content categories after approval\n- future modular product/package design\n\n## Relationship to other models\n\n### M1\u2013M5 launch milestones\n\nM1\u2013M5 are the internal Block Ops launch milestones.\n\nThey describe how Block Ops itself gets built, tested, validated, and launched.\n\nThey are not the same as the six-pillar Wiki structure.\n\n### Client Delivery Lifecycle \u2014 provisional\n\nThe current 8-phase client lifecycle remains available as **Client Delivery Lifecycle \u2014 provisional**.\n\nIt keeps client-delivery content together for now, but it should not become the main Wiki architecture. Later, delivery content can be reviewed and reorganized under the six pillars.\n\n### Older product-outline taxonomies\n\nOlder pillar models and product outlines should be preserved as historical/source material only.\n\nIf they contain useful language, that language can be selectively merged into the current six-pillar model, but the older taxonomy should not be treated as current Wiki truth.\n\n## Current standard / process\n\nWhen classifying a Wiki page, source document, deliverable, or Mission Control context link:\n\n1. Use the six-pillar structure first.\n2. If the item fits multiple pillars, choose the primary pillar and add secondary links only when useful.\n3. Do not resurrect older pillar names unless Samir explicitly approves them.\n4. Keep claim-heavy content under Value Intelligence and mark it for evidence review.\n5. Keep final/client-facing content separate from internal draft material.\n6. Treat Implementation Bundles as the productized materials layer, not as a catch-all for every Wiki page.\n\n## Related Mission Control workflows/tasks\n\n- Wiki migration / Foundation identity batch\n- Wiki navigation buildout\n- Mission Control task-to-Wiki linking\n- Implementation bundle review\n- Modular product/package strategy\n\n## Related Wiki pages\n\n- [[Block Ops Positioning and Core Story]]\n- [[Block Ops Mission]]\n- [[Block Ops Vision]]\n- [[Block Ops Wiki Classification Rules]]\n- [[Block Ops Wiki Master Content Inventory]]\n- [[Implementation Bundles]]\n- [[Client Delivery Lifecycle - provisional]]\n\n## Open questions / needs update\n\n1. Samir side note: some pillar names may need to be renamed later so they are more intuitive. Do not rename now; revisit after the first Wiki buildout pass.\n2. Should \u201cImplementation Bundles\u201d stay as the sixth pillar name, or should it eventually be renamed for client-facing clarity?\n3. Should modular sales packages map exactly to the six pillars, or should they use smaller named modules under the pillar structure?\n4. Which pillar should own billing/documentation if it becomes a small standalone commercial module: Value Intelligence, Implementation Bundles, or a named submodule under both?\n5. Are the pillar subtitles right: The Engine, The System, The People, The Partners, The Proof, The Productized Materials?\n",
