    "content": "# Block Ops Operating System\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Block Ops operating system is the internal structure that keeps the company from running on memory, scattered chat, or one-off effort.\n\nIt connects four layers:\n\n1. **Wiki / Obsidian** \u2014 source-of-truth knowledge, decisions, SOPs, scripts, and durable context.\n2. **Mission Control** \u2014 live execution board for milestones, workflows, owners, statuses, blockers, and next actions.\n3. **Dashboard** \u2014 calm executive summary of what matters now.\n4. **Client portal** \u2014 approved, client-safe output only.\n\nThe goal is simple: every important piece of work should have a place, an owner if it is actionable, a current status, and enough context that the team can continue without guessing.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nBlock Ops is building a repeatable site-level regional anesthesia program model. That cannot depend on everyone remembering the latest conversation.\n\nThe operating system protects against:\n\n- task drift\n- duplicate work\n- unreviewed drafts becoming \u201cfinal\u201d\n- client-facing material leaking before approval\n- unclear ownership\n- build decisions getting separated from the business reason behind them\n- Mission Control becoming a task dump instead of a useful execution layer\n\n## How it works\n\n### 1. Milestones define the major gate\n\nCurrent internal launch milestones are:\n\n- **M1 \u2014 Mock Client Lead-to-Live Run:** prove the mock-client path can move from lead capture through mock go-live.\n- **M2 \u2014 Foundational Client Ready:** prove Block Ops can responsibly accept a real foundational client.\n- **M3 \u2014 External Validation Completed:** trusted attendings/reviewers pass the review/test.\n- **M4 \u2014 Foundational Client Successfully Completed:** complete the first foundational client successfully.\n- **M5 \u2014 Paid Client Successfully Onboarded:** onboard a paid client successfully.\n\nMilestones are team goals, not owned objects.\n\n### 2. Workflows organize the work inside a milestone\n\nA workflow is a lane of related work, such as Lead Capture, Outreach Sequence, Proposal Workflow, Training Day Scheduling, or QA / Validation.\n\nWorkflows do not need equal task counts. A workflow should exist because it makes the work easier to understand, not because the board needs symmetry.\n\n### 3. Tasks are the executable unit\n\nTasks are the only launch objects that need owners.\n\nA good task should answer:\n\n- what needs to be done\n- who owns it\n- what status it is in\n- what workflow it belongs to\n- what source page explains the context\n- what proves it is done\n\nSplit a task when it bundles multiple owners, deliverables, systems, decisions, tests, or client paths.\n\n### 4. Source pages explain why the work exists\n\nMission Control should not carry all context. Each meaningful task should link back to one primary Wiki page or SOP that explains the operating logic.\n\nThe page carries the \u201cwhy\u201d and \u201chow.\u201d Mission Control carries the \u201cwhat next.\u201d\n\n## Current standard / process\n\n### Operating loop\n\n1. Capture a decision, rule, workflow, or task from source material or conversation.\n2. Route it to the correct layer:\n   - Wiki / Obsidian for durable knowledge\n   - Mission Control for execution\n   - SOP for repeatable process\n   - client portal only if approved and client-safe\n3. If it creates action, create or update the Mission Control task.\n4. If it changes the operating rule, update the Wiki page or SOP.\n5. If it changes a milestone gate, update the milestone definition and related tasks.\n6. Close the loop by stating which layers changed.\n\n### Layer separation\n\n| Layer | Purpose | Should contain | Should not contain |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Wiki / Obsidian | Internal source of truth | pages, SOPs, decisions, scripts, source context | live-only task state as the only record |\n| Mission Control | Execution tracker | tasks, owners, statuses, priorities, dependencies | long explanations that belong in Wiki pages |\n| Dashboard | Calm summary | current milestone, priorities, blockers, next actionable work | every task on the board |\n| Client portal | Client-facing home | approved final content and client-safe status | drafts, internal strategy, legal drafts, private notes |\n\n## Related Mission Control workflows/tasks\n\n- M1 Mock Client Lead-to-Live Run\n- M1 Final System Test\n- Dashboard / Mission Control UX\n- SOP / Knowledge Organization\n- Operating Rhythm\n- QA and Validation\n\n## Related wiki pages\n\n- [[Mission Control Rules]]\n- [[Roles and Ownership]]\n- [[Weekly Ops Review]]\n- [[Decision Capture]]\n- [[Status Rules]]\n- [[M1 Mock Client Lead-to-Live Workflow]]\n- [[Block Ops Wiki Master Content Inventory]]\n\n## Open questions / needs update\n\n- Confirm whether M3/M4/M5 naming should be displayed exactly this way in the internal Wiki or kept as plain milestone descriptions until final launch polish.\n- Add live Dashboard screenshots or examples later once Max\u2019s internal dashboard flow stabilizes.\n- Link each Mission Control workflow to one primary Wiki page after the first Wiki buildout pass is complete.\n",
